course: biology
lesson: 243
title: Animal builders: termite mounds, weaver birds and other nest makers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some of the best builders on Earth have no hands at all, only jaws, beaks and spit.
explain: In Africa, termites a few millimetres long build mounds taller than a grown man out of soil, spit and droppings; for a termite that is like people building a tower kilometres high.
explain: The termites do not live at the top: they live in the nest below, and the mound works like a pair of lungs and an air conditioner, letting stale air out and fresh air in.
explain: Birds are builders too: a weaver bird ties real knots in grass, a swallow sticks hundreds of beakfuls of mud under your roof, and a bowerbird builds a stage just to show off.
explain: Each building solves a problem: keeping cool, keeping eggs away from snakes, or winning a mate.
fact: Some African termite mounds are several metres high, built by insects only a few millimetres long from soil, spit and droppings, with chimneys and tunnels for air.
fact: The termites live in the nest underground; air moves through the mound as the day warms and cools, and the Eastgate Centre in Zimbabwe was designed with cooling ideas from termite mounds.
fact: Magnetic termites in Australia build thin, flat mounds pointing north and south: the wide sides catch the weak morning and evening sun, and the thin edge faces the hot midday sun.
fact: Many termites grow fungus gardens on chewed wood and plants inside the mound, and the fungus helps to break down the wood; a termite queen can be as big as a finger and lay thousands of eggs a day.
fact: Male weaver birds weave hanging nests from grass strips, tying knots with beak and feet; nests at the tips of thin branches are hard for snakes and monkeys to reach, and if the female does not like a nest the male pulls it down and starts again.
fact: Sociable weavers in the Kalahari build giant shared nests like haystacks in trees, with many rooms and over 100 birds; the thick straw keeps them cool by day and warm on freezing desert nights, and a nest can last over 100 years.
fact: Swallows and house martins build cup nests from mud mixed with spit, and a house martin nest takes about 1,000 mud pellets.
fact: Male bowerbirds in Australia build a bower of sticks, which is not a nest but a stage to impress females, and some decorate it with blue flowers, berries and bottle tops.
fact: The tailorbird pokes holes in leaves and pulls plant fibres or spider silk through them to sew a leaf cup round its nest.
fact: Some swiftlets build nests almost entirely from their own hardened spit, and people collect them for bird's nest soup, an expensive food in China; the nests are costly because they must be collected carefully from cave walls so the birds can still breed.
fact: The Australian malleefowl lays its eggs in a huge heap of leaves and sand that warms up as it rots; the father tests the heat with his beak and adds or removes sand, and the chicks dig out alone.
fact: Real woodpeckers drill holes to nest and to find insects, and drum on wood to call; their skull is built to soften the blows, and they can hit wood about 20 times a second.
exercise: How tall can some African termite mounds be? | Several metres, taller than a man | About as tall as a pet cat sitting up | About as tall as a mountain | Never taller than a pencil
exercise: What do termites build their mounds from? | Soil, spit and droppings | Stones carried from rivers | Leaves sewn together with silk | Wax made inside their own bodies
exercise: Where do the termites of a big mound mostly live? | In the nest down below, not at the top | Right up at the very top of the tall mound, in the chimney | On the outside walls of the mound | In trees near the mound
exercise: What does the tall mound above the nest do? | It works like lungs, swapping stale air for fresh | It stores rain for the long dry season | It is a lookout tower where guards watch for anteaters | It is a trap for birds
exercise: Which building copied cooling ideas from termite mounds? | The Eastgate Centre in Zimbabwe | The Eiffel Tower in Paris, France | The Great Wall of China | The pyramids of Egypt by the Nile
exercise: Why do magnetic termites point their flat mounds north and south? | So the thin edge faces the hot midday sun | So the termites can find their way home | So the wind blows the mound down | So rain runs into the nest
exercise: What do many termites farm inside their mounds? | A fungus garden | Rows of tiny plants | Herds of ants for milk | Bees for their honey
exercise: How does a male weaver bird make its nest? | It ties grass strips into knots with its beak and feet | It digs a long tunnel deep into a sandy river bank with its beak | It glues sticks together into a cup with mud | It takes over an old woodpecker hole
exercise: Why do weaver nests hang from the tips of thin branches? | Snakes and monkeys find them hard to reach | The wind rocks the chicks to sleep | The tips are the warmest part of the tree | The birds cannot fly to the trunk
exercise: What does a male weaver do if the female does not like his nest? | Pulls it down and builds a new one | Builds a bigger nest next door to the first | Moves into the female's own nest instead | Gives up nesting for the rest of the year
exercise: How is a sociable weavers' nest different from most bird nests? | It is a huge shared nest with many rooms | It is built deep under the ground, below the tree's roots | It is made only of soft feathers it has found | It is used for one single night and then abandoned
exercise: How many mud pellets does a house martin nest take? | About 1,000 | Only about 10 | About 100,000 | Only about 50
exercise: What is a bowerbird's bower for? | A stage to impress females | A nest for eggs | A store for winter food | A trap for insects
exercise: How does a tailorbird make its nest? | It sews leaves together with fibres or spider silk | It knits dry grass into a deep cup using only its long toes | It hollows out a fruit and lives inside it | It builds a cup of stones
exercise: What are the nests used in bird's nest soup made of? | The swiftlet's own hardened spit | Seaweed woven together tightly | Rice straw and mud | Chicken feathers and moss stuck with honey
exercise: How does a malleefowl keep its eggs warm? | It buries them in a heap of rotting leaves and sand | It sits on them day and night until they hatch | It keeps them in a hot spring | It hides them under its wings while it flies about
exercise: Why can a woodpecker hammer wood without hurting itself? | Its skull is built to soften the blows | It wears down its beak to nothing every week | It only ever hits very soft, rotten wood | It has no brain at all inside its little head
test: In a cartoon a woodpecker drills a row of holes in a man's house just to annoy him. Why do real woodpeckers drill? | To make nest holes, find insects and drum to call | Only to annoy the people who live nearby | To sharpen their beaks so they can fight rivals | To store their eggs safely inside the wood
test: «went up the termite mound in the film, there's no termites at the top lol, they all moved out?» What is right? | No, they live in the nest below and the mound is their air conditioning | Yes, the mound is empty, and all the termites moved out long ago to a new place | Yes, the termites all died long ago | No, they are hiding in the roof in the daytime
test: «6 termites. how many legs. 6 x 6 innit» | 36 | 24 | 48 | 12
test: «3 swallows on the wire, 2 more land, how many legs then» | 10 | 5 | 20 | 12
test: What would happen if a bowerbird's bower had no blue things? | Females would be less likely to choose him | His eggs would not hatch in the nest | The bower would fall down in the first rain shower | He would be unable to fly
test: It is a dry spring with no puddles anywhere. Which bird will find it hardest to build? | The house martin, which needs mud | The weaver bird, which uses grass | The woodpecker, which drills wood | The bowerbird, which uses sticks
test: «why'd that weaver bird rip his own nest up, idiot» What is the best answer? | The female did not like it, so he starts again | He was angry at a snake that had climbed into the nest | He needed the grass to eat | Weaver birds never build nests
test: A haystack hangs in a desert tree in the Kalahari. Who lives there? | Over a hundred sociable weavers in separate rooms | A single huge eagle and its chick | A family of monkeys | Nobody, because farmers store their hay up there for the winter
test: Why are the nests in bird's nest soup so expensive? | They are made of swiftlet spit and must be collected carefully so the birds can still breed | They are made of thin gold leaf, beaten flat by craftsmen in little workshops by the sea | They are grown in labs | They come only from penguins
test: A malleefowl's heap is getting too hot in summer. What does the father do? | Removes some of the sand | Lays more eggs | Pours water on it | Leaves the eggs to cook
test: «how can tiny bugs build a house that big, no way» | Millions of termites each add a little soil and spit | A queen termite builds it alone, one grain at a time | The wind piles up the soil | Elephants help them build it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principal function of the elevated portion of a termite mound? | It effects the exchange of stale and fresh air for the colony housed beneath | It accommodates the entire population of the colony, the queen dwelling at its summit | It serves exclusively as a lookout for the detection of predators | It is a structure abandoned by the termites upon its completion
exam: Identify the principal reason for which magnetic termites align their mounds upon a north-south axis. | The minimisation of exposure to the intense midday sun | The facilitation of navigation by foraging workers | The collection of rainwater from the prevailing winds | The attraction of mates from distant colonies
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the bower constructed by the male bowerbird. | It constitutes a display ground for the attraction of females and is not employed as a nest | It is the chamber wherein the eggs are incubated by both parents in turn throughout the spring | It is a store for food in times of scarcity, chiefly berries and insects | It is erected jointly by the male and the female
exam: Ascertain the material of which the nests harvested for bird's nest soup are predominantly composed. | The hardened saliva of the swiftlet | Woven grasses bound with mud | Feathers shed by the parent birds | Seaweed gathered from the shore at low tide
